// The size of an allocated array is a number.
//
// Two of the three places an array size is written asked that: a declarator
// (`int32 fixed[n]`) and an array literal. The third -- `new T[n]`, which is
// the one people write -- evaluated the size and threw the answer away.
//
//     int32[] a = new int32["hello"];
//     println(format("len={}", a.length));    // len=-1250607064
//
// No diagnostic, no crash, and a length read out of whatever the pointer
// happened to be. Every index into it is then bounds-checked against garbage.
// Only the first dimension was even evaluated, so the rest were unexamined
// too.
//
// Zero stays legal here, and that is the difference from the declarator form:
// `new int32[0]` is an array with nothing to read, which
// tests/array-zero-initialization.abs relies on, while a declarator's storage
// is the frame's and has to have a size.

int32 main() {
    // A string, a floating value and a bool are not sizes.
    int32[] fromText = new int32["hello"];
    int32[] fromReal = new int32[1.5];
    int32[] fromFlag = new int32[true];

    // Nor is an array, which is what the prefix bug used to let through.
    int32[] source = new int32[2];
    int32[] fromArray = new int32[source];

    return fromText.length + fromReal.length + fromFlag.length + fromArray.length;
}
